00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
hi there steve coffin here  today i want to talk about context the  importance of context in language  learning  context is a bit big part of our our  learning process in my opinion  because it's also so important to  whether we can understand something um  i'll give you  very quickly a few examples um when i  was in st petersburg about five six  years ago and i had been studying  russian  and i went to buy a ticket in the train  station  uh i of course waited in line and then i  arrived uh at the counter there and the  lady said something to me that i had not  anticipated  her saying i i didn't have the context  and so i couldn't understand what she  was saying even though i knew the words  i  just caught me by surprise  if i had been a regular you know  visitor to the train station buying  tickets i would understand everything  that she said  but i wasn't used to it i must say that  i did much better in la vie ukraine  maybe my ukrainian was better but also i  had a better sense of what the person  behind the counter  was saying  another example even in my own language  i was at uh whole foods which is store  as a store i never go to  uh to buy brown sugar cubes for my  coffee  and i didn't know that at whole foods  uh when you check out  at the cash register they ask you and if  you have your own bag like i bring my  own shopping bag  they ask you if you want 10 cents off  your purchase or whether you want to  contribute to a charity  and because  i didn't realize this i didn't have the  context  the girl at the cash register said you  want 10 cents off or you want to convert  your journey  i didn't understand what she was saying  and she had i asked her to repeat it  about two or three times  and then she explained the context that  they have the system she assumed that i  was a regular shopper when she explained  that to me then i understood what she  was saying so context is very important  to our comprehension  context is also important to  learning and i think you know recently  i've been talking about motivation i've  been talking about the importance of  repetition and novelty both i've been  talking about the importance of having  you know simple tasks that you can just  do without worrying about how you're  doing uh i tend to avoid any sort of  language learning tasks which involve me  trying to think or to remember or get  something right i just do these tasks  repetitively whether it be listening or  even you know and i'll get into this  doing some of our  flash cards and other activities as i  turn the page and link  but always i do stuff that relates to a  context  and that is why for example i found the  uh  the assimil book for arabic  which i was persuaded to buy when i was  in bratislava by a person who works for  the german assimil and he said this was  the best book and i in fact someone here  at youtube suggested the same  but what i found in there is that as  they introduce the language and  introduce a lot of explanation and stuff  it's always based on sort of one simple  you know sentence  the man went in the house the boy roped  the man sat down  which there's no context so i i find it  very tough  sledding as we say very hard to continue  in that book so i really have not spent  any time with the assimil book  whereas when i do our mini stories now  people are going to criticize me you  know slamming asimil promoting link i'm  sorry that's what i do i do what i think  works for me so the many stories that's  a context now it's not  totally  exciting content like stephen crash and  talks about compelling content  i still find that at the beginning stage  we kind of have to deal with  a context that is still a context  there's a little mini story there  it's meaningful to me because i'm trying  to understand it and i'm trying to  understand it when i hear it  so that it's a challenge for me to do  that so to that extent it is a context  and it's meaningful but it's not  something that is is tremendously  exciting it's not like reading about the  history say of uh i don't know  syria or something in arabic which  hopefully one day i'll be able to do  so context  is how i like to learn and i think it's  uh  i i don't worry  i don't worry about what i remember  don't remember  as long as i stay in the context  uh and i think our brains work that way  we need that context another example i  was driving this morning in the fog here  in vancouver and all of a sudden i  wasn't so certain about  where i should turn because normally  when there's no fog i see i get all  these hints from trees and buildings and  stuff so automatically i know where to  go when a lot of that sort of  surrounding context is taken away  then it's more difficult so i i think  the words that come together with other  words are very useful content context  for our comprehension and so i think  it's quite legitimate to do most of our  learning in some kind of a meaningful  context so for example in my arabic  learning  and i should say it's  why i'm talking so much about my arabic  learning is that you know in learning  other languages you know slavic  languages romance languages obviously i  have an advantage because i have learned  other slovak slavic or romance languages  but maybe  me learning arabic is like someone who  is learning a second language for the  first time where everything is new and  difficult and different so with arabic i  have a different writing system i have  you know essentially no very very few  vocabulary items that are common to  languages that i know  and so i'm finding it very useful to  stay in the context of our many stories  and to do what we call sentence view  which is what i do uh on the computer or  it's only on the browser version so i  can't do it on my app  but i look at it sentence by sentence i  i highlight the words that i need to  look up or to remember  and then i will highlight two or three  of them together to create a little  phrase and i'll often highlight the  whole sentence so that i can hear  the text to speech of the whole sentence  so i can hear the text to speech of the  individual word of a short phrase and of  the whole sentence and then as i turn  the page at link move to the next  sentence of course i have to do  these activities so again simple tasks  like i spoke about earlier simple tasks  that we just do so for me the flash  cards i just put all the flash card  stuff on the front page i don't on the  front page of the flashcard i don't  scratch my brain but i look at it  um  you know listen to the text to speech  um  sometimes i you know there's five  different activities i've got you can  change the settings you can change which  activities you do you can change what's  on the front page the back page of the  flash card there are a number of  settings so i vary it a variety again i  vary it and i go through the word the  phrase sometimes the sentence in these  activities but i am doing this review  activity closely related to the context  of the sentence and the sentence in turn  is tied to the context of the story so  everything is contextually linked it's  part of something bigger so i find that  more satisfying as a learning  activity  it helps me understand  and i recognize that perhaps i  understand the word in this context and  when i next run into the word i may have  forgotten it or it may have a different  meaning in a different context it  doesn't matter it's just this continuous  exposure to the language in a meaningful  context  with repetition and novelty  and simple tasks that repeat  and i i just feel myself becoming more  powerful in arabic certain you know i'm  over a hundred known words according to  my statistics  um there's probably more than that but  i'm a bit reluctant to move them there  just yet  um  some of the stuff that i saw  in my little arabic grammar that i have  leafed through a couple of times i'm  starting to pick up pick up on those you  know whether it's a  you know the verb whether it's a he or a  she or an i or a u and and some of the  endings of the nouns depending on  whether it's my book or something all  i'm starting to see or a book the book  i'm starting to see these things  but here again  our objective at link is to have grammar  notes for each of these sentences so  that the grammar note would be tied to  the specific context where it occurred  in the future we're also having planning  to make it possible to to basically have  the natural voice sound  attached to that sentence so those are  some of the things that we want to  introduce but in the meantime even as it  is i find it very useful it is sort of a  context based learning activity and i  am enjoying my arabic and i feel that  i'm making progress and i think  at least for me and i can't speak for  everyone at least for me learning in a  meaningful context is very important so  thank you for listening and i look  forward to your comments bye for now  [Music]  you

